CAT = "cpaaudSpecial"; SEG = "SPC"
Q = [
("When inventory is material, AU-C 501 requires the auditor to attend the physical count unless it is:",
 "Impracticable to do so", ["Tracked on a perpetual system", "Counted by the client", "Insured against loss"],
 "If attendance is impracticable, the auditor performs alternative procedures. If those cannot give sufficient evidence, the opinion is modified for a scope limitation."),
("During the inventory count, the auditor traces a sample of tags to the final inventory listing. This tests:",
 "Completeness", ["Existence", "Valuation", "Rights and obligations"],
 "Going from the floor to the listing shows that counted items made it into the records. Going from the listing to the floor tests existence."),
("Inventory held by a third-party warehouse is material. The auditor would generally:",
 "Confirm it with the warehouse", ["Rely on the client's records", "Exclude it from testing", "Count it at the client"],
 "AU-C 501 calls for confirmation with the custodian, or inspection or other procedures when the inventory is material."),
("The primary purpose of a letter of inquiry to the client's legal counsel is to:",
 "Corroborate management's information", ["Estimate the year's legal fees", "Find any unrecorded revenue", "Test the lawyer's own independence"],
 "The letter asks the lawyer to confirm or comment on the information management gave about litigation, claims and assessments."),
("The client's lawyer refuses to respond to the auditor's inquiry about a material lawsuit. This is:",
 "A scope limitation", ["An emphasis-of-matter", "A going concern issue", "An other-matter issue"],
 "A refusal by legal counsel to respond appropriately is a limitation on the scope of the audit, which may lead to a qualified opinion or a disclaimer."),
("When auditing investments measured at fair value using a pricing service, the auditor should understand:",
 "How the service sets prices", ["The service's staff count", "The service's fee", "Where the service is based"],
 "The auditor evaluates the methods and inputs the pricing service uses, particularly for securities without active market quotes."),
("Under AU-C 570 for a nonissuer, the auditor evaluates going concern for a period of one year after:",
 "The date the statements are issued", ["The balance sheet date", "The date the auditor's report is released", "The end of the first quarter"],
 "SAS 132 aligns with U.S. GAAP: the reasonable period is one year after the date the statements are issued, or available to be issued. PCAOB AS 2415 uses one year beyond the balance sheet date."),
("Which condition most likely raises substantial doubt about going concern?",
 "Default on a loan covenant", ["Launch of a new product line", "A change of the company's auditors", "An increase in dividends paid"],
 "Negative trends, loan defaults, loss of a key customer and legal proceedings are conditions that may indicate substantial doubt."),
("Management's plans alleviate the auditor's substantial doubt about going concern. For a nonissuer, the auditor:",
 "Needs no going concern section", ["Must disclaim an opinion", "Must give an adverse opinion", "Must add a qualification"],
 "When substantial doubt is alleviated by management's plans, the report has no going concern section, but the auditor considers the adequacy of related disclosures."),
("Substantial doubt about going concern remains after considering management's plans, and disclosure is adequate. For a nonissuer, the opinion is:",
 "Unmodified, with a separate section", ["Qualified for the going concern doubt", "Adverse on the statements", "A disclaimer in every case"],
 "The opinion remains unmodified, with a separate section titled Substantial Doubt About the Entity's Ability to Continue as a Going Concern."),
("Misstatements that arise from management's judgments about estimates the auditor finds unreasonable are called:",
 "Judgmental misstatements", ["Factual misstatements", "Projected misstatements", "Clearly trivial items"],
 "Factual misstatements are beyond doubt, judgmental ones arise from unreasonable estimates or policy choices, and projected ones are extrapolated from samples."),
("The auditor accumulates misstatements found during the audit and should then:",
 "Ask management to correct them", ["Book the adjusting entries itself", "Report them to the SEC", "Disclose them in the auditor's report"],
 "The auditor communicates accumulated misstatements to the appropriate level of management on a timely basis and requests their correction."),
("Uncorrected misstatements total just below materiality. The auditor should most likely:",
 "Consider undetected misstatements", ["Issue an adverse opinion immediately", "Ignore them as immaterial", "Lower materiality to zero"],
 "When the total approaches materiality, the risk that undetected misstatements push it over is higher, so the auditor considers more procedures or asks for corrections."),
("Written representations from management should be dated:",
 "As of the auditor's report date", ["As of the balance sheet date", "On the date of the engagement letter", "On the report release date"],
 "AU-C 580 requires the representations to be dated as of the date of the auditor's report, and covers all periods referred to in it."),
("Who should normally sign the management representation letter?",
 "The CEO and CFO", ["The audit committee chair", "The internal auditor", "The controller's staff"],
 "Representations come from management with overall responsibility for financial and operating matters, usually the chief executive and chief financial officers."),
("Management refuses to give a written representation that it has provided the auditor with all relevant information and access. The auditor of a nonissuer should:",
 "Disclaim or withdraw", ["Issue an unmodified opinion", "Add an other-matter paragraph", "Ask the lawyer instead"],
 "Under AU-C 580, if management will not confirm its responsibilities for preparing the statements and providing information and access, the auditor disclaims an opinion or withdraws when permitted."),
("Written representations from management are:",
 "Necessary but not sufficient", ["Sufficient evidence alone", "Optional for nonissuers", "A substitute for testing"],
 "Representations are required audit evidence but do not provide sufficient appropriate evidence on their own about any matter."),
("Which procedure covers the period between the balance sheet date and the auditor's report date?",
 "Reading minutes of later meetings", ["Confirming year-end receivables balances", "Testing year-end controls", "Observing the inventory count"],
 "Subsequent events procedures include reading later minutes and interim statements, inquiring of management and inquiring of legal counsel."),
("A subsequent event occurs after the date of the auditor's report but before the report is released, and the statements are adjusted for it. The auditor may:",
 "Dual-date the report", ["Date it at year-end", "Leave the event out", "Issue two reports"],
 "With dual dating, the report keeps its original date except for the note on the later event, which carries its own date. The auditor's responsibility for later events is limited to that note."),
("After the report is released, the auditor learns of a fact that existed at the report date and would have changed the report. The auditor should first:",
 "Discuss it with management", ["Notify all users directly", "Withdraw the old report alone", "Ignore it, as it is released"],
 "For subsequently discovered facts, the auditor discusses the matter with management and determines whether the statements need revision and how management will tell users."),
("After the report is released, the auditor finds that a necessary procedure was omitted. If the omission impairs the ability to support the opinion, the auditor should:",
 "Perform the procedure now", ["Recall the report at once", "Notify the state board", "Do nothing further"],
 "For an omitted procedure, the auditor assesses its importance and, if it impairs support for the opinion, performs it or alternative procedures promptly."),
("Fixed assets increased sharply this year. The auditor's best evidence about additions is to:",
 "Vouch them to invoices", ["Recompute depreciation", "Review the repairs account", "Confirm with the bank"],
 "Vouching additions to purchase invoices and contracts supports their existence and cost."),
("Reviewing the repairs and maintenance expense account mainly helps find:",
 "Capital items expensed", ["Fictitious fixed assets", "Overstated depreciation", "Missing bank deposits"],
 "Large items in repairs may be improvements that should have been capitalized, so the review addresses completeness of fixed assets."),
("To test the valuation of inventory, the auditor would compare costs with:",
 "Recent selling prices", ["The count sheets", "Shipping documents", "The tag number sequence"],
 "Comparing cost with net realizable value or later selling prices shows whether a write-down is needed."),
("In an audit of an employee benefit plan, the plan's investment information is certified by a qualified institution. Under SAS 136 the auditor may perform:",
 "An ERISA 103(a)(3)(C) audit", ["A review instead", "No procedures on investments", "A compilation of the plan"],
 "SAS 136 replaced the limited-scope audit with the ERISA section 103(a)(3)(C) audit, in which the auditor still evaluates the certified information and reports an opinion."),
("A client's controller has unusual journal entries reversing just after year-end. These most likely signal:",
 "Window dressing at year-end", ["Normal monthly accruals", "Errors made by the bank", "Sampling error in testing"],
 "Entries booked at year-end and reversed soon after can inflate results or improve ratios for one date, so the auditor investigates their business purpose."),
("The auditor obtains a confirmation of a client's long-term debt from the lender. It mainly supports the debt's:",
 "Existence and terms", ["Classification as current", "Interest expense accuracy", "Approval by the board"],
 "The lender's confirmation gives independent evidence of the balance, interest rate, collateral and other terms."),
("A company has a significant investment in a joint venture accounted for by the equity method. Good evidence of the carrying amount is:",
 "The investee's audited statements", ["A broker's quote for the investee's shares", "Management's own estimate", "Last year's carrying amount"],
 "Audited statements of the investee support the share of earnings and the carrying amount. Unaudited statements need more procedures."),
]
